  "read": {
    "headline": "The hike landed, the bond market blinked first, and the board bought the dip.",
    "standfirst": "Wednesday's unanimous 25bp took the S&P down 0.45% and the Dow 1.21%. Thursday gave most of it back: the S&P rose 1.14% to 7,637.76, its largest gain since early August, the ten-year fell to 4.94% from a 5.04% Tuesday high, and WTI is heading for its first weekly loss in three. The board below is Thursday's close with Friday morning's commodities, FX and crypto.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "The belly still owns the month; the long end owns the week.",
        "text": "Over a month the two- and three-year yields are up 11.46% and 11.76% while the thirty-year is up 1.93% — the repricing of the policy path has not gone anywhere. But since the hike the long bond is the one part of fixed income in the green: TLT is up 1.11% on the day and 1.13% on the week while the short-duration SHY is flat. A curve that steepened into the meeting and flattened out of it is a market that thinks the Fed is now doing the work the long end had been doing alone. The 2s10s is 27bp."
      },
      {
        "title": "Technology carried Thursday, and nothing else did.",
        "text": "XLK rose 2.25% on the day against a flat financials sector (−0.09%) and staples up 0.19%. Over six months the gap is a chasm: technology is up 35.85%, consumer discretionary 1.54%, utilities down 10.42%. Thursday's rebound was a semiconductor rebound — the growth factor beat the index by 0.52% while small caps trailed it by 1.11% — which is a narrower recovery than the headline index suggests."
      },
      {
        "title": "Korea and Taiwan are the same trade at different volumes.",
        "text": "EWY rose 3.90% and EWT 2.07% on the day, both riding the chip rally. Year to date they are up 87.61% and 74.15% respectively, yet Korea is down 16.79% over three months and Taiwan up 0.59%. Same driver, but one of them has already had its correction and the other has not. The won weakened 3.25% against the dollar this week even as the index rallied, which is the part worth noticing."
      },
      {
        "title": "Crude fell five percent and energy stocks did not.",
        "text": "WTI is down 5.46% on the day to $96.35 and 3.70% on the week as Saudi Arabia reroutes exports and says half the East-West pipeline returns within days. XLE rose 0.70%. Year to date WTI is up 67.07% and the energy sector 44.22% — the equity has lagged the commodity all year and is not giving back much as it eases. Heating oil, up 129.23% year to date, says the tightness is in distillates rather than crude."
      },
      {
        "title": "The yen fell on the day the BoJ hiked.",
        "text": "USD/JPY rose 1.18% to 157.79 after the Bank of Japan raised to 1.25%, and is up 2.85% on the week. A hike that was fully priced and delivered with two dissents is a hike the market sells. Elsewhere the dollar is simply bid: EUR/USD is down 1.11% on the week and GBP/USD 1.12% after the Bank of England's 6–3 hold, while USD/CHF is up 3.53% on the month."
      },
      {
        "title": "Softs are unwinding while grains keep going.",
        "text": "Cocoa fell 9.02% on Thursday and is down 8.75% on the week; coffee is down 15.42% on the month. On the other side of the agricultural grid, corn is up 12.18% on the month and wheat 22.26% over three months. Bitcoin, for its part, rose 2.18% to $78,012 and is up 12.57% on the month, still down 12.09% on the year."
      }
    ]
  },
